{"name":"Looking Glass","hex":"#888786","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"looking glass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of looking-glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/looking_glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Countdown to Looking Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Countdown to Looking Glass is a Canadian made-for-television movie that premiered in the United States on HBO on October 14, 1984 and was also broadcast on CTV in Canada. The movie presents a fictional confrontation between the United States and the Soviet Union over the Strait of Hormuz, the gateway to the Persian Gulf. The narrative of the film details the events that lead up to the initial exchange of nuclear weapons, which was triggered by a banking crisis, from the perspective of an ongoing news broadcast."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b8/Countdown_to_Looking_Glass_Cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Countdown_to_Looking_Glass","wikidata":"Q5176861"},{"title":"Looking Glass (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Looking Glass is a 2018 American thriller film directed by Tim Hunter and starring Nicolas Cage and Robin Tunney. It was released in the United States by Momentum Pictures on February 16, 2018."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/11/Looking_Glass_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Looking_Glass_(film)","wikidata":"Q48965014"},{"title":"Looking Glass (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Looking Glass is an American pop rock band formed in New Jersey that were active during the early 1970s. They are known for their chart-topping 1972 hit song \"Brandy \", which reached No. 1 on both the U.S. Billboard Hot 100 and Cash Box Top 100 charts, remaining in the top position for one week. They were part of the Jersey Shore sound."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Looking_Glass_(band)","wikidata":"Q1869698"},{"title":"Mirror","blocks":[{"text":"A mirror, also known as a looking glass, is an object that reflects an image. Light that bounces off a mirror forms an image of whatever is in front of it, which is then focused through the lens of the eye or a camera. Mirrors reverse the direction of light at an angle equal to its incidence. This allows the viewer to see themselves or objects behind them, or even objects that are at an angle from them but out of their field of view, such as around a corner. Natural mirrors have existed since prehistoric times, such as the surface of water, but people have been manufacturing mirrors out of a variety of materials for thousands of years, like stone, metals, and glass. In modern mirrors, metals like silver or aluminium are often used due to their high reflectivity, applied as a thin coating on glass because of its naturally smooth and very hard surface."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror","wikidata":"Q35197"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG/500px-Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG/1280px-Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG/500px-Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A first-surface mirror coated with aluminium and enhanced with dielectric coatings. The angle of the incident light (represented by both the light in the mirror and the shadow behind it) exactly matches the angle of reflection (the reflected light shining on the table).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEnhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg/500px-WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg/500px-WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"4.5-metre (15 ft)-tall acoustic mirror near Kilnsea Grange, East Yorkshire, UK, from World War I. The mirror magnified the sound of approaching enemy Zeppelins for a microphone placed at the focal point. Sound waves are much longer than light waves, thus the object produces diffuse reflections in the visual spectrum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea(PaulGlazzard)Jan2007.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Egypt%2C_New_Kingdom%2C_Dynasty_18_-_Caryatid_Mirror_-_1983.196_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Egypt%2C_New_Kingdom%2C_Dynasty_18_-_Caryatid_Mirror_-_1983.196_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Egypt%2C_New_Kingdom%2C_Dynasty_18_-_Caryatid_Mirror_-_1983.196_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Egypt%2C_New_Kingdom%2C_Dynasty_18_-_Caryatid_Mirror_-_1983.196_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg?utm_so